# Findings the bridge image cannot take a fix for, with what would lift each.
# Read by the image scan in .github/workflows/node-ci.yml. Every other
# fixable HIGH or CRITICAL still fails that job.
#
# All three live in npm's own bundled dependencies, under
# usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm/node_modules/, pinned by npm's lockfile.
# No npm release ships any of these fixes: 12.0.2 is the newest and was
# measured carrying all three. Patching a dependency inside npm's own tree
# is not something an image should do to itself, and npm cannot simply be
# removed — the bridge launches its backends through `npx`, so npm is
# runtime code here rather than build tooling.
#
# What reaches these is a package an operator names through `add_backend`,
# and running that package is arbitrary execution by design; the bridge
# exists to do it. A denial of service, or a parser disagreeing about an
# address, sits below what that caller already commands. This reasoning
# does not extend to the bridge's own dependencies, none of which has ever
# appeared in these findings.

# brace-expansion 5.0.7, in npm's bundled tree.
# Lift when an npm release bundles 5.0.9 or later.
CVE-2026-14257
CVE-2026-69152

# tar 7.5.19, in npm's bundled tree: denial of service on a crafted
# long-path archive, which npm would meet while extracting a package.
# Lift when an npm release bundles 7.5.21 or later.
CVE-2026-73566

# ip-address 10.2.0, in npm's bundled tree: inconsistent parsing between
# it and other consumers of the same string.
# Lift when an npm release bundles 10.3.1 or later.
CVE-2026-69192
